, 2446, 2566, 2670, 2671)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Multiple absolute contraindications limit 磺达肝癸钠 use in high-risk populations\",\n            \"explanation\": \"磺达肝癸钠 is contraindicated in several well-defined patient populations due to heightened risk of adverse outcomes. These include patients with severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance <20 mL/min), clinically significant active bleeding, acute bacterial endocarditis, and known hypersensitivity to the drug or its excipients. Additionally, caution is advised in patients with elevated bleeding risk, such as those with congenital or acquired bleeding disorders. These restrictions are critical for safe prescribing and reflect the drug’s potent anticoagulant mechanism [Data: Relationships (7003, 7004, 7005, 7006, 7007, 7008); Entities (14392, 14481, 14490, 14605, 965, 14660)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"磺达肝癸钠 interacts with other anticoagulants and fibrinolytics, amplifying bleeding risk\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Concomitant use of 磺达肝癸钠 with other agents that increase bleeding risk—such as unfractionated heparin (UFH), low molecular weight heparin (LMWH), heparin-like drugs, and fibrinolytic agents—is discouraged. These combinations can potentiate anticoagulant effects and significantly elevate the risk of hemorrhagic complications. This interaction profile necessitates careful medication reconciliation and avoidance of overlapping antithrombotic therapies unless under strict clinical supervision [Data: Relationships (47224, 47227, 51669); Entities (10016, 9869, 11494, 7723)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Anticoagulant class as a whole is associated with significant bleeding and thrombotic risks\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The broader 抗凝剂 (anticoagulant) drug class is linked to both major and clinically relevant non-major bleeding events, as well as thrombus recurrence, particularly in complex patient populations such as those with cancer-associated thrombosis (CAT). A study of 267 CAT patients found that 42.7% experienced drug-drug interactions (DDIs) with anticoagulants, though DDI presence did not significantly alter rates of thrombosis recurrence or bleeding. Nonetheless, the baseline risk remains substantial, underscoring the need for vigilant monitoring [Data: Relationships (11941, 11943, 6367); Entities (10894, 15788, 15352, 17328)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Coagulation indicators are critical for monitoring anticoagulant therapy\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Monitoring coagulation parameters is essential when administering anticoagulants, especially in patients on interacting medications. For instance, warfarin therapy requires regular assessment of凝血指标 (coagulation indicators), including the international normalized ratio (INR), particularly when co-administered with drugs like miconazole that inhibit CYP2C9 and potentiate warfarin’s effect. Similarly, patients receiving复方盐酸阿替卡因 (articaine hydrochloride compound) while on anticoagulants must have INR closely monitored due to bleeding risk [Data: Relationships (1077, 1618); Entities (13368, 4743)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Other drugs in the community exhibit distinct but related safety profiles\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Drugs like 尼达尼布 (nintedanib) and 复方盐酸阿替卡因 are part of this pharmacovigilance network due to their associations with thrombotic and hemorrhagic events. Nintedanib has been evaluated for risks of venous and arterial thromboembolism, though studies (e.g., INPULSIS trials) showed no increased risk of venous thromboembolism. However, it is still flagged for potential bleeding and arterial thromboembolic events in structured safety data. These findings place nintedanib within the broader context of drugs requiring thrombosis and bleeding risk assessment [Data: Relationships (11442, 11434, 11435); Entities (5216, 17679, 15596)].\"\n       